Z||000000||BENGAL4||07-07-2001||01:31 PM||||Hey guys,<P>I'm back from Canada, and all I can say is WOW. This thing is so fast it scares me. <P>Now to my problem. The ac compressor seized and I am putting another one in today and I need help with the belts and tensioners. Will someone direct me with the routing? Maybe there is a website that can help.<P>Thanks in advance,<P><BR>Bill||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phil||07-07-2001||05:09 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/3800belt.jpg"> <P>That's how mine is anywho.||||reg||1
Z||000002||terryk||07-07-2001||06:45 PM||||What year engine? The Series I (92-95) is different from the Series II (96-01).<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||BENGAL4||07-07-2001||11:12 PM||||Terry,<P>It is a 97 engine. How do you put the belts on? I have never done this with a serpentine belt?<P>Thanks,<P>Bill||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||07-08-2001||12:50 AM||||Use a 15mm wrench to hold the tensioner back. It's usually the easiest to route the belt around all of the pulleys, idlers and tensioners except the ALT for the rear belt and blower for the front belt. Then rotate the tensioner AS FAR AS POSSIBLE and slip the belt over the ALT or blower. Trying to slip the belt over the tensioner idler almost never works. You invariably get shaky hand.<P>Notice that from the top of the AC to the top of the ALT is one long run with no idlers inbetween.<P>Check that all of the belts are seated in the pulleys.<P>Terry<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/s2scbelts.jpg">   <P>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 07-08-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 07-08-2001).]||||reg||1
